---
import Capability from '../layouts/Capability.astro';

// Every technical claim here is something a CTO can test in the first call, so
// nothing goes on this page that cannot be defended. The apps named are the
// ones publicly listed with the entitlement.
const sections = [
  {
    heading: 'What the Screen Time API actually is',
    body: [
      `Apple exposes app blocking through three frameworks that only work together.
       <strong class="text-slate-300">FamilyControls</strong> gets permission and hands you opaque tokens
       instead of bundle IDs. <strong class="text-slate-300">ManagedSettings</strong> applies the shield.
       <strong class="text-slate-300">DeviceActivity</strong> runs the schedules and thresholds that decide when.`,
      `The tokens are the part people trip over. You never learn which app the user picked.
       You cannot read the name, you cannot read the icon, you cannot log it to your backend.
       That is deliberate, and it means the usual analytics and debugging habits do not apply.
       Anything you want to know about a blocked app has to be derived on the device.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'The parts that are genuinely hard',
    body: [
      `<strong class="text-slate-300">Getting the entitlement.</strong> Family Controls is a restricted
       entitlement. You submit a request explaining the use case and Apple reviews it by hand.
       Vague answers get refused. I have been through this approval six times, and the request
       that gets approved reads like a product spec, not a form.`,
      `<strong class="text-slate-300">The extension sandbox.</strong> DeviceActivityMonitor and
       ShieldConfiguration run in separate extensions with tight memory limits and no debugger
       worth the name. They wake, do a small amount of work, and die. Shared state goes through
       an App Group, and anything expensive gets you terminated mid-callback.`,
      `<strong class="text-slate-300">Shields that fire late, or not at all.</strong> Most bug reports
       here trace back to threshold events being coalesced, a monitor never restarting after
       reboot, or a schedule crossing midnight in the wrong time zone. Understanding the
       difference is the whole job.`,
      `<strong class="text-slate-300">Review.</strong> Screen Time apps get scrutinised harder than
       average, particularly around 5.1.1 and anything that reads as surveillance. Writing the
       submission so the reviewer understands the intent up front saves weeks.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Beyond the standard block screen',
    body: [
      `ShieldConfiguration lets you replace Apple's grey shield with your own, and that is where
       most of the product lives. I have built a shield that shows a snail whose health improves
       the less you scroll, a lockdown armed by physically tapping an NFC tag, and a shield tied
       to daylight exposure so a screen limit lifts once you have actually been outside.`,
      `The mechanics are the easy half. The hard half is making a limit feel like something the
       user chose rather than a punishment, because the ones that feel punitive get uninstalled
       in a week.`,
    ],
  },
];

const apps = [
  {
    name: 'Shelly',
    what: 'Gamified screen time. Apple approved the Family Controls entitlement by hand; shields are drawn with ShieldConfiguration and usage thresholds fire through DeviceActivityMonitor.',
    url: 'https://apps.apple.com/us/app/shelly-screen-time-control/id6757734582',
  },
  {
    name: 'Block',
    what: 'Tap the phone on a physical NFC tag and the distracting apps disappear. Tap again to bring them back. Core NFC starts the session, Family Controls enforces it.',
    url: 'https://apps.apple.com/app/id6752846663',
  },
  {
    name: 'Bright Start',
    what: 'A circadian reset where the screen limit lifts only after the camera and light sensor confirm real daylight exposure.',
    url: 'https://apps.apple.com/app/id6745139907',
  },
  {
    name: 'Chapter',
    what: 'Habit replacement. Rather than only blocking, it substitutes the blocked moment with something else and tracks the streak.',
    url: 'https://apps.apple.com/us/app/chapter-replace-a-bad-habit/id6757711400',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can you get the Family Controls entitlement approved for my app?',
    a: 'I can write and submit the request, and I have had it approved six times. Approval is Apple\'s decision, not mine, so nobody can promise it. What I can say is that refusals almost always come from a vague use case, and the fix is describing exactly what the app does, who it is for, and what data never leaves the device.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can the app see which apps a user blocked?',
    a: 'No. FamilyControls returns opaque tokens, not bundle identifiers. You cannot read the name or icon of a selected app, and you cannot send that selection to a server. Any product idea that depends on knowing which specific apps a user picked needs rethinking before development starts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this work on Android too?',
    a: 'Not with these frameworks. Screen Time is Apple-only. Android has its own APIs with different rules and a different permission model. If you need both, they are two builds, and pretending otherwise is how timelines slip.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does my shield sometimes appear late?',
    a: 'Usually the DeviceActivityMonitor extension was terminated or the threshold event was coalesced by the system. Occasionally it is a schedule crossing midnight in a time zone you did not test. It is nearly always diagnosable, but it needs on-device testing rather than the simulator.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you fix an existing Screen Time app rather than build a new one?',
    a: 'Yes, and that is most of the work people bring me. Shields not firing, monitors dying after reboot, an entitlement request that keeps getting refused, or a rejection at review. A fixed-scope diagnosis first is usually cheaper for you than an open-ended engagement.',
  },
];
---

<Capability
  title="iOS Screen Time & Family Controls Developer — Muhammad Asad"
  description="Freelance iOS engineer specialising in the Screen Time API: Family Controls, ManagedSettings, DeviceActivityMonitor and ShieldConfiguration. Six approved entitlements and four shipped App Store apps."
  path="/screen-time-ios-developer/"
  crumb="Screen Time & Family Controls"
  h1="iOS Screen Time & Family Controls Developer"
  lede="I build app-blocking and digital wellbeing apps on Apple's Screen Time API. Six Family Controls entitlements approved by Apple, and shipped apps using ManagedSettings, DeviceActivityMonitor and custom shields."
  sections={sections}
  apps={apps}
  faqs={faqs}
/>
